Date: 8 Feb 93 02:58:07 GMT
From: ssandfort@attmail.com
Subject: META: Burden of proof reform

sssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssss

Tom Morrow recently posted a proposal that would place the
"burden of proof" on those who challenge basic Extropian tenets.
As Tim May pointed out in a subsequent post, this idea dovetails
with my "Great Debate" suggestion of a week or two back.

I think Tom's proposal is excellent, as far as it goes.  What
seems to be missing is an enforcement mechanism.  I seriously
doubt that challenging newbies will spontaneously shoulder this
burden.  So what can we do to see that they do?

NOTHING, IGNORE IT--This is Tim May's suggestion.  In most cases,
this is probably the best way to handle things.  Eventually, most
of the "young Turks" mellow out.  Most, but not all.

"PUT-UP-OR-SHUT-UP" CHALLENGES--Some provocations are hard to
ignore.  Some provocations *impossible* to ignore.  List members
must decide for themselves where they would set those boundaries.
But when in the course of human events . . . (sorry I got carried
away there).  But when a list member decides a provocative post
should be challenged, the Extropian list rules should facilitate
that action.  Tentatively, I would suggest that any list member
who challenges another would have to explicitly state which
tenets of the list are being denied.  The list administrator
could informally mediate the challenge to make sure that it is
substantive.  If the administrator thinks the challenge has
merit, the party who is challenged would have to "put up" some
evidence supporting their position.  If they failed to do so,
they would lose posting privileges (at least on that subject).
If evidence were posted, it would, of course, be subject itself
to further discussion, refutation and debate.

FORMAL DEBATES--I can foresee circumstances when the evidence
posted in response to a challenge would be inadequate, in error,
or even manufactured.  In such cases, the appropriate solution
would be a formal debate along the lines of my previous post.  I
know such debates won't decide Ultimate Truth.  But they should
let the parties have their say, yet put a lid on it thereafter.

Whatever we decide, though, the "burden of proof" idea should not
be dismissed out of hand.  Some form of "burden of proof" limits
to access can be of immeasurable help in avoiding flame wars,
reducing the noise level and preserving our on-line enclave for
Extropian fellowship.

