Date: Sun, 7 Feb 93 21:40:41 PST
From: ghsvax!hal@uunet.UU.NET (Hal Finney)
Subject: ECON: Zero-sum investments

I have a few more thoughts on the issue of futures, risk, and return.
As Robin suggested, I picked up some books on the subject at the library
today and I'm just going through them.  There does seem to be some
disagreement about how large the average returns are in commodity
futures "investing" (e.g. speculating).  One book even claims that
average returns are negative, perhaps because investors are risk-
seeking, and so they are willing to pay for their fun.  I need to
look at them in more detail.

It seems to me that one problem is definitional.  How do you calculate
the percentage return on a futures transaction?  Absolute return is
easy - it's whatever price changes occured from the time you entered
a position to when you closed it out.  But for a percentage or fractional
return, you have to divide by the base investment.

But there really isn't a well-defined base investment amount in a futures
contract - not that I can see, anyway.  The amount you have to put in
to "control" the contract is a small fraction of the total value of
the contract - typically 5 or 10 percent.  And that is really an arbitrary
fraction, determined to some extent by politics.  It can be thought of
as "earnest money" to prove that you are serious about your commitment.
Theoretically, if an exchange trusted a valuable customer, they could
conceivably require 0% margin and just trust him to make good (ignoring
legal restrictions).

So I don't think it makes sense to divide your gains by the amount the
exchange or brokerage made you put into your margin account.  It doesn't
seem right, either, to divide by the total value of the contract, since
you certainly don't have to put in that much money.  I really don't
know what you should divide by, so I'm not sure how you would calculate
the return on a futures contract.

Robin sent me a recent draft of his Idea Futures paper, and it seemed
quite good, much improved from what I remember reading a year or more
ago.  He emphasizes there that people can make "bets" denominated not
only in cash, but in any financial instrument - a stock, a mutual fund,
gold, bonds, or whatever.  So the average rate of return between the
two people betting against each other is equal to the rate of return
of the underlying instrument.

I do see a problem with this, though.  It seems that the total return
can't _quite_ be as high as that of the financial instrument, since the
betting exchange has to take a cut.  So the return will be a little less.
And the risk seems somewhat, possibly quite a bit, greater than the
risk of just holding the instrument - after all, there may well be a
better than 50-50 chance that you will lose all of your investment.

So, you have greater risk and less return than the underlying instrument,
so it seems economically irrational for at least one of the two sides
to be participating in this transaction.  (This analysis is based, I
think, on the theory known as Capital Asset Pricing Models, CAPM,
which basically says that rate of return should be related solely to
the risk level of an investment.  One of the books I got claimed that
CAPM doesn't really fit the data very well in general, and that it
is completely wrong for futures investments.  Again, I have to look
at it more closely.)

Hal

