From: D Anton Sherwood <dasher@well.sf.ca.us>
Subject: BOOK: the impossibility of history

Dale Worley's cookie file contains this unusually long quotation, from
someone whose narratives leave many questions unanswered:

> Nothing frustrates me more than the twentieth century adherence to the
> notion that you can find out what "actually happened" and that it is
> necessary for fiction to set out a linear, quantitative and absolute
> reality for the reader's consumption and assurance.
> 
> I think EVERYTHING is like the Kennedy assassination(s); riddled with
> inconsistencies, false trails overlapping stories and considerations;
> distortions wrapped inside fabrications and coated with lies. The
> sooner we get over the idea that reality isn't like this, the sooner
> we'll be able to put together a world that fits our circumstances as
> they are; not as they never were and will never be. I'm not holding my
> breath.
> 
> -- Dave Sim

Kim Stanley Robinson's novel ICEHENGE (Ace 1985, I think) is in three parts.
 The first is about rebellion, boundless expansion, and the downside of
hiding one's opinions.  The second is about archaeology and glasnost.  The
third is about archaeological hoaxes.

The first segment, 2248, is the diary of Emma Weil, who seems to belong to
the first generation to receive, as a child, the immortality treatment.  Her
trade is closed-biosphere engineering, and she helps a secret starship
project.  She also takes part in the "Washington-Lenin Alliance" to free
Mars from a Soviet-style state.  The starship people plan to take advantage
of the rebellion to make their getaway.  Weil breaks off the diary as she is
about to go to ground in the Martian desert.

The second segment, about 2360, has Professor Hjalmar Nederland digging in a
ruined city.  For a century the official story has been that the city's
people blew the dome for crazy reasons of their own, and that there never
was a Washington-Lenin Alliance.  The state is loosening up a bit, and now
it is possible -- at least for a "safe" and well-connected scholar like
Nederland -- to investigate.  In the city, Nederland has a flash of memory:
he was here, ten years old, when the state blew the dome.  (After a
lifetime, events are mostly forgotten.)  In a vehicle buried outside the
city, his team finds the diary of Emma Weil, which is the first anyone's
heard of the starship.  Meanwhile, a monument is found on Pluto, inscribed
"2248".

Part three:  about 2435, Nederland's great-grandson casts doubt on the
authenticity of the Weil diary (which was found in circumstances which would
look suspicious to a suspicous mind) and the date of the monument.  He
thinks he knows who built the monument -- but maybe she is Emma Weil with a
new name.

The reader is left wondering, Can we know the past?  And does it matter?

